MOUSE(4)                       P32W Unix                        MOUSE(4)

NAME
       mouse - the video card's PS/2 mouse, for a program

SYNOPSIS
       /dev/mouse   character device 8, minor 0

       #include <sys/mouse.h>

       read(fd, packets, 3 * n)       n packets (unsigned char)

DESCRIPTION
       The console has no use for the mouse: it is off. While a
       program has /dev/mouse open it is on, and read() gives what
       it sends, a packet of three bytes each time it is moved or
       a button changes:

       byte 0    the buttons that are down: MS_LEFT (1), MS_RIGHT
                 (2), MS_MIDDLE (4); bit 3 is always 1; MS_XNEG
                 (0x10) and MS_YNEG (0x20) are the signs of the
                 two movements
       byte 1    how far it has moved to the right since the last
                 packet: with MS_XNEG, 256 less (to the left)
       byte 2    how far up; with MS_YNEG, 256 less (down)

       MS_DX(p) and MS_DY(p) are the two movements of the packet
       at p, as numbers with their signs. The mouse counts its own
       steps: nothing here knows of the screen. gmouse (gfx(3))
       keeps a pointer on the screen from them.

       read() waits for a packet and then gives as many whole ones
       as have come and fit; a count below 3 is an error. select(2)
       says whether one is waiting, which is how a program waits
       for the mouse and the keyboard together, or does not wait.
       64 packets are kept for a program that is slow to read;
       more are lost.

       Turning the mouse on takes the card's PS/2 section about
       half a second, in which the keyboard waits too; open()
       does not wait for it. When the device is closed, or the
       program ends, the mouse is off again.

       One program at a time: a second open fails with EBUSY. Only
       root and group staff may open the device. It does not need
       /dev/video: a program that only writes text may have the
       mouse.

EXAMPLES
       /usr/examples/mouse.c
              a cross that follows the mouse (with gfx(3))

       unsigned char p[3];
       int fd = open("/dev/mouse", O_RDONLY);
       while (read(fd, p, 3) == 3 && !(p[0] & MS_RIGHT))
           printf("%d %d\n", MS_DX(p), MS_DY(p));
              each movement, until the right button

ERRORS
       ENXIO   no video card
       EBUSY   another program has the mouse
       EINVAL  a count below 3
       EINTR   a signal while waiting for a packet

FILES
       /dev/mouse

SEE ALSO
       gfx(3), video(4), select(2)

       docs/video-card-acrtc.md

LIMITATIONS
       The packets are the plain PS/2 ones: no wheel, three
       buttons. A mouse that is not plugged in sends nothing, and
       nothing says so.
